7,4,7,5,3,8,5,4,1,1,5,8,5,3,9,6,4,8,9,7],[7,9,0,2,5,1,9,3,8,0,2,1,8,7,1,0,0,3,2,4,9,2,8,6,7,5,7,7,6,9],[9,0,8,7,2,4,0,3,4,1,8,8,0,0,0,9,1,2,1,0,2,9,7,8,0,2,7,7,4,1],[4,8,4,1,9,7,3,9,2,9,5,4,8,5,1,7,6,8,8,2,6,3,4,0,2,8,6,4,2,5],[3,9,4,3,4,2,8,5,5,2,6,5,5,5,9,1,6,8,9,7,6,0,9,3,7,3,1,4,4,0],[3,0,6,3,6,9,4,3,9,9,0,2,6,3,8,0,6,3,0,0,8,1,8,0,0,4,2,8,4,4],[3,9,3,9,6,7,0,8,0,1,9,3,0,7,5,6,6,4,0,9,4,8,3,8,0,0,4,0,0,5],[7,6,5,5,3,8,5,1,6,1,7,4,9,3,8,8,7,4,1,4,4,9,0,0,0,0,3,0,0,0],[4,7,7,8,8,5,0,3,5,0,7,3,1,9,9,6,6,6,0,8,9,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0],[7,7,4,2,0,1,8,9,2,2,2,5,2,0,9,5,9,1,1,0,0,1,0,0,1,6,8,4,0,0],[0,6,4,3,6,9,9,5,6,9,8,9,6,5,7,1,9,5,3,1,9,5,0,0,8,1,6,5,8,2],[6,8,0,0,5,9,5,9,0,0,7,0,0,0,8,4,3,9,5,4,7,3,0,0,2,3,3,7,0,0],[3,1,2,1,0,8,9,5,9,5,7,2,8,3,1,8,0,8,7,3,0,8,0,0,1,9,7,2,0,0],[3,8,0,0,0,1,4,3,7,0,9,1,7,9,2,3,7,0,2,3,5,2,0,0,0,0,0,0,0,0],[6,7,1,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,6,5,0,0,2,0,5,2,2,6,2,0,0,0,0,0,0,0,5],[5,2,1,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,0,0,8,3,1,1,7,3,3,5,4,0,0,1,0,4,5,5,1],[9,5,0,4,4,2,2,2,2,4,4,8,5,6,8,8,7,9,3,2,7,2,0,8,8,2,0,2,0,8],[6,9,1,4,4,2,4,4,4,4,4,3,8,8,0,7,6,5,5,8,0,0,8,1,3,3,3,9,4,1],[9,6,6,4,4,2,4,4,4,4,4,9,9,9,2,5,4,1,7,4,5,4,6,0,6,6,0,4,5,5]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The first submission matched pairs 1 and 2 exactly. In pair 3 it got one cell wrong: (4,4) should be 8. That cell is 7 in the input, which is the block colour itself. It sits inside the crack in the 7-block. In the crack window, rows 1–5 and columns 2–5, it looks like this:\n\n\n\nAll four neighbours of (4,4) are crack cells: 1 above, 8 below, 9 to the left, 0 to the right. So it is a pocket of block colour that is fully enclosed by the crack, and it belongs to the crack shape. Compare (2,4), which is also 7. Its upper neighbour (1,4) is part of the open 7-block, and in the expected output it stays 7. So the fix is this: after finding a crack, also fill any component of block colour *c* that lies wholly inside the crack. Such a component touches nothing except crack cells and does not reach the grid edge. In pairs 1 and 2 no such pocket exists, so those results stay the same.\n\n**The full rule:**\n1. Look at every colour *c*, the background included.\n2. A crack is a connected group of at least 3 cells that are not *c*. Every in-grid neighbour of it must be *c*, and exactly one of its cells must lie on the grid border.\n3. Any *c*-pockets enclosed entirely by the crack also belong to it.\n4. Repaint the whole crack in the colour of its border cell.\n\nThe program emits one stage per crack filled, then the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    out = g.copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    dirs = ((1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1))\n    for c in range(10):\n        if not (g == c).any():\n            continue\n        same_comps = [comp for comp in components(g == c) if len(comp) <= 10]\n        for comp in components(g != c):\n            if len(comp) < 3 or len(comp) > 40:\n                continue\n            cs = set(comp)\n            ok = True\n            for y, x in comp:\n                for dy, dx in dirs:\n                    ny, nx = y+dy, x+dx\n                    if 0 <= ny < H and 0 <= nx < W and (ny, nx) not in cs and g[ny, nx] != c:\n                        ok = False\n                        break\n                if not ok:\n                    break\n            if not ok:\n                continue\n            edge = [(y, x) for y, x in comp if y in (0, H-1) or x in (0, W-1)]\n            if len(edge) != 1:\n                continue\n            col = int(g[edge[0]])\n            # pockets of block colour fully enclosed by the crack\n            fill = list(comp)\n            for sc in same_comps:\n                ss = set(sc)\n                enclosed = True\n                for y, x in sc:\n                    if y in (0, H-1) or x in (0, W-1):\n                        enclosed = False\n                        break\n                    for dy, dx in dirs:\n                        q = (y+dy, x+dx)\n                        if q not in ss and q not in cs:\n                            enclosed = False\n                            break\n                    if not enclosed:\n                        break\n                if enclosed:\n                    fill.extend(sc)\n            for p in fill:\n                out[p] = col\n            emit(out, f\"Crack inside the colour-{c} block reaches the grid edge at {edge[0]} with colour {col}; fill the whole crack (and any block-colour pockets it encloses) with {col}.\")\n    emit(out, \"All edge-touching cracks filled with their edge colour.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"So it is a pocket of block colour that is fully enclosed by the crack, and it belongs to the crack shape."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Any *c*-pockets enclosed entirely by the crack also belong to it."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"ches the grid edge at (10, 24) with colour 9; fill the whole crack (and any block-colour pockets it encloses) with 9."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"01a67210185b1e1dbd304b2346cfd7351772f186265b6fbec97959cb9c949a37","task_sha256":"6ca4caf8a413a14365cc683e7b162e0bf95856f81f2b5e2deac5d6a585b5034d","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
